{
  "image_name": "4a85514c7d9cb500bb6cbc6d5f3ca82d.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two broad roles of lysosomal cathepsins in cancer cells: promoting survival on the left and driving cell death on the right. On the survival side, extracellular proteins are taken up by macropinocytosis, trafficked through endosomes to lysosomes, and degraded by cathepsins such as CTSS, CTSL, and CTSB into free amino acids. These nutrients feed mTOR signaling and support cell growth and proliferation. The same side also shows lysosomal biogenesis and autophagolysosome formation, suggesting that cathepsin activity helps maintain the degradative system that recycles cellular material. In parallel, CTSZ appears linked to integrin-mediated signaling at the plasma membrane, activating SRC/FAK pathways that also favor survival and proliferation.\n\nThe death side shows a switch in outcome when lysosomal integrity is lost. Reactive oxygen species or lipid peroxidation seem to trigger lysosomal membrane permeabilization (LMP), releasing cathepsins into the cytosol. These enzymes can activate BID to tBID, which promotes mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization (MOMP) and apoptosis. A separate route indicates that cathepsin-containing autophagolysosomes may contribute to autophagic cell death. Overall, the image emphasizes that lysosomal cathepsins can either sustain tumor cell fitness or initiate lethal pathways depending on context.",
